<Guide slug="praying-on-a-plane" title="Praying on a plane" description="When to pray during a flight, standing or seated, and how to face the qibla on board, with sources and where scholars differ." lede="Prayer has set times, and a flight doesn’t pause them. Most of the time you can plan around the flight. When you can’t, the sources make room for you to pray on board as well as you are able." sources={sources}>
  <h2>First, try to pray on the ground</h2>
  <p>Prayer is prescribed at set times <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 4:103)</span>. On the ground you can usually stand, bow, prostrate and face the qibla properly, so that is the best place to pray when the time allows.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If a prayer’s time has come in before boarding, pray it at the airport. Our <a href="/airports/">airport pages</a> show where the prayer rooms are.</li>
    <li>If the prayer’s time will still be running when you land, you can pray it after landing.</li>
    <li>If you follow a school that allows combining on a journey, you may be able to pray two prayers together on the ground, before boarding or after landing. The Prophet ﷺ combined Ẓuhr with ʿAṣr, and Maghrib with ʿIshāʾ, while travelling <span class="cite">(Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 1111, 1106)</span>. The Ḥanafī school does not allow this outside Ḥajj. See <a href="/travel/shortening-and-combining/">shortening and combining</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When the time falls in the air</h2>
  <p>If a prayer’s whole time passes while you are flying, and you can’t pray it before or after, pray it on the plane rather than let it go. Allah does not charge a soul beyond its capacity <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 2:286)</span>, and we are told to fear Him as much as we are able <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 64:16)</span>.</p>
  <p>Prayer times in the air follow the sun where the plane actually is, not the clock at home or at your destination. Flying east shortens the day and flying west stretches it, so a prayer may come sooner or later than you expect. Many travellers check the flight map for the sunset or dawn, or ask the crew.</p>

  <h2>Standing or seated</h2>
  <p>The Prophet ﷺ told a man who was unwell: “Pray standing; if you can’t, sitting; and if you can’t, on your side” <span class="cite">(Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 1117)</span>. Scholars apply the same order on a plane.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>If you can stand,</b> stand for an obligatory prayer. Al-Nawawī reports that on a ship the obligatory prayer may not be prayed sitting by someone able to stand, and that Mālik and Aḥmad held the same; Abū Ḥanīfah allowed sitting on a moving ship, though standing is better <span class="cite">(al-Majmūʿ 3:242; al-Hidāyah 1:77)</span>. A report that the Prophet ﷺ said of praying on a ship, “pray standing unless you fear drowning,” is in al-Dāraquṭnī and al-Ḥākim; al-Albānī graded the version from Ibn ʿUmar ṣaḥīḥ, while another version has a weak narrator <span class="cite">(dorar.net)</span>. Some travellers ask the crew, politely, whether there is a safe place the crew can designate for a few minutes. If the crew say no, or the seatbelt sign is on, follow their instructions.</li>
    <li><b>If you can’t stand,</b> pray in your seat. Bow by leaning forward, and lean a little further for prostration, so that it is lower than the bow <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:76; Minhāj p. 24)</span>. Dizziness or similar is counted as an excuse for sitting <span class="cite">(al-Majmūʿ 3:242)</span>.</li>
    <li><b>If you can stand but can’t bow or prostrate fully,</b> the schools differ. The Ḥanafī text says standing is then no longer required, and praying seated with gestures is better <span class="cite">(al-Hidāyah 1:77)</span>; ask your local scholar what your school says.</li>
    <li><b>Voluntary prayers</b> can be prayed seated in your seat.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The qibla on a plane</h2>
  <p>We are told to face the Masjid al-Ḥarām wherever we are <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 2:144)</span>. On a plane, the flight map shows where you are; from there you can work out roughly which way Makkah lies, or ask the crew.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Voluntary prayers:</b> the Prophet ﷺ prayed voluntary prayers on his mount facing whichever way it went. For an obligatory prayer, he got down and faced the qibla <span class="cite">(Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 400)</span>. Al-Nawawī lists voluntary prayer on a journey as an exception to facing the qibla, and adds that a rider who can easily face the qibla does so at the start <span class="cite">(Minhāj p. 24)</span>. Scholars today apply this to voluntary prayers on a plane.</li>
    <li><b>Obligatory prayers:</b> facing the qibla is a condition for anyone able to face it, and the exception for riders is for voluntary prayer only <span class="cite">(Minhāj p. 24)</span>. On a ship, al-Nawawī says that if the wind turns the ship and your face turns away from the qibla, you must turn back to it and carry on <span class="cite">(al-Majmūʿ 3:242)</span>; Ibn Qudāmah likewise says a rider who can face the qibla must do so, “like one on a ship” <span class="cite">(al-Mughnī 1:316)</span>. So the plane turning is different from being truly unable to face the qibla. For real inability, scholars point to the verses on ability above <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 2:286; 64:16)</span>, and some also cite “to Allah belong the east and the west” <span class="cite">(Qurʾān 2:115)</span>. Ask your local scholar how your school treats a seat you can’t turn in.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where scholars differ</h2>
  <p>Scholars differ on whether an obligatory prayer prayed seated or away from the qibla because of the flight has to be repeated after landing. The classical discussion is about prayer on a ship or a mount, and scholars today apply it to planes. In the Shāfiʿī school, an obligatory prayer may not be prayed on a moving mount <span class="cite">(Minhāj p. 24)</span>; a traveller who cannot get down without being cut off from his group prays on the mount “for the sake of the time” and repeats it, though al-Nawawī notes that al-Qāḍī Ḥusayn gave two views on the repeat <span class="cite">(al-Majmūʿ 3:242)</span>. Others say a prayer prayed as well as you were able is enough. Ask your local scholar which view to follow.</p>

  <h2>Practical tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Make wuḍūʾ just before boarding. If you put on khuffs or socks after wuḍūʾ, many scholars allow wiping over them later <span class="cite">(Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī 206)</span>; the conditions are on <a href="/travel/wudu-at-the-airport/">wuḍūʾ at the airport</a>.</li>
    <li>A window seat makes praying in your seat easier and quieter for your neighbours.</li>
    <li>Note the prayer times along your route before you fly.</li>
    <li>Keep it short and calm. Travel prayers are short, and the crew will appreciate it.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
